{
  "policy_id": "ld-129-new-right-to-affordable-legal-assistance",
  "party": "Liberal Democrat",
  "policy_title": "New Right to Affordable Legal Assistance and Reform Legal Aid",
  "outcome": "O9",
  "verdict": {
    "outcome": "O9",
    "direction": "improves",
    "magnitude": "minor",
    "time_horizon": "this-parliament",
    "confidence": "low",
    "plain": "Expanding legal aid and creating a right to legal assistance would help more people access due process and equal treatment under the law, especially vulnerable groups currently locked out by 'advice deserts'. But the policy text commits no budget or binding mechanism, so how much improvement actually materialises is highly uncertain.",
    "biggest_unknown": "Whether meaningful new funding is committed \u2014 past incremental investments (e.g. \u00a38m in 2019) were negligible against the scale of cuts, and without a funded delivery mechanism the 'new right' may not translate into real access.",
    "claims": [
      {
        "tier": "stated",
        "claim": "The policy promises to establish a new right to affordable legal assistance and reform legal aid to be simpler, fairer and more generous.",
        "evidence_id": "M",
        "quote": "Establishing a new right to affordable, reasonable legal assistance, and making the Legal Aid system simpler, fairer and more generous."
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "By 2024, 3.5 million people lived in areas with no active civil legal aid provider.",
        "evidence_id": "E5",
        "quote": "By 2024, 3.5 million people lived in areas with no active civil legal aid provider, and 38 local authorities lacked any provider"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Cuts to legal aid disproportionately impacted vulnerable groups including children, people with learning difficulties, domestic abuse victims and low-paid migrants.",
        "evidence_id": "E6",
        "quote": "Past cuts disproportionately impacted vulnerable individuals, including children, people with learning difficulties, victims of domestic abuse, benefit claimants, and low-paid migrants"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Amnesty International described the result as a 'two-tier civil justice system' where access is limited to those who can afford it.",
        "evidence_id": "E29",
        "quote": "civil legal aid cuts had \"decimated access to justice\" and created a \"two-tier civil justice system\" where access is limited to those who can afford it"
      },
      {
        "tier": "measurable",
        "claim": "Civil legal aid case volumes fell by approximately 57% between 2013 and 2024.",
        "evidence_id": "E31",
        "quote": "Civil legal aid case volumes fell by approximately 57% and real expenditure by nearly 15% between 2013 and 2024"
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "A previous \u00a38 million funding commitment was described as little more than 2% of the annual cuts, suggesting the scale of genuine reform required is very large.",
        "evidence_id": "E35",
        "quote": "Resolution cautiously welcomed an \u00a38 million funding commitment in 2019, but noted it was \"little more than 2% of the \u00a3350 million of annual cuts\" and urged for \"meaningful funding\""
      },
      {
        "tier": "projected",
        "claim": "Removal of early legal advice has been linked to escalation of problems such as rent arrears leading to eviction, suggesting that restoration would reduce such harms.",
        "evidence_id": "E2",
        "quote": "The removal of early legal advice has been linked to the escalation of legal problems, such as rent arrears leading to eviction"
      }
    ],
    "rationale": "Access to legal assistance sits at the core of O9: without affordable legal help, equal treatment before the law and meaningful due process are unavailable to people of modest means. The evidence shows that post-LASPO cuts created large-scale advice deserts (3.5 million people with no local provider) and systematically disadvantaged vulnerable groups \u2014 both direct O9 harms. A policy reversing this trajectory by establishing a right to legal assistance and expanding legal aid scope clearly points in the direction of improvement on equal treatment and due process. However, two constraints limit the verdict to 'minor' with low confidence. First, the policy text contains no committed budget, no statutory duty with enforcement mechanism, and no quantified target \u2014 'establishing a right' and 'making the system more generous' are aspirational without a delivery instrument. Second, the evidence on past reform attempts shows that small investments (\u00a38m cited against \u00a3350m annual cuts) made negligible difference. The policy earns a direction of 'improves' because a legal right, once enacted, is a real instrument \u2014 unlike purely aspirational language \u2014 and the evidence base on what legal aid deprivation does to equal access is strong and consistent. But magnitude is held to 'minor' because the gap between the stated ambition and the scale of the problem is vast, and no funding commitment is cited that would close it materially within a parliament.",
